1
00:00:00,471 --> 00:00:02,554
[SPEAKER_00]: When is the right time to go into solitude?

2
00:00:03,215 --> 00:00:04,136
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, kind of thing.

3
00:00:04,656 --> 00:00:10,823
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think it's the same for whether you're a layperson or a monk.

4
00:00:11,244 --> 00:00:17,911
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to be able to be a... you could say, a friend to yourself, in a way.

5
00:00:18,612 --> 00:00:19,473
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to be able to...

6
00:00:21,624 --> 00:00:23,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Live with yourself, in ease, with ease.

7
00:00:24,205 --> 00:00:31,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be virtuous and sensual strength, and not have this... a mind full of desire, and hatred, and confusion.

8
00:00:33,146 --> 00:00:47,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you have that, which is basically having... being... living with an enemy, if you... if you have that kind of mind, which is your enemy, and you go and stay in solitude, well, you are going to have a difficult time.

9
00:00:48,031 --> 00:00:50,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to have a mind which is your friend.

10
00:00:52,352 --> 00:00:54,853
[SPEAKER_00]: And... and any...

11
00:00:56,993 --> 00:01:12,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a mind with any of the qualities that a friend would have, that you would consider someone who would be a friend, you know, kind, compassionate, not desirous, lustful, averse, complaining all the time, anxious and so on.

12
00:01:15,209 --> 00:01:19,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you... how do you develop that kind of mind towards yourself?

13
00:01:20,852 --> 00:01:34,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you've got to, to quite a degree, bring those lust, aversion, and distraction, confusion, you have to bring that to an end by being virtuous.

14
00:01:34,537 --> 00:01:38,498
[SPEAKER_00]: You're no longer doing things you know that are bad for you.

15
00:01:42,381 --> 00:01:51,191
[SPEAKER_01]: What if... what if somebody can't help but breaking... keep breaking the precepts and losing sense of strength?

16
00:01:54,895 --> 00:01:57,939
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's going to happen anyway.

17
00:01:58,519 --> 00:01:58,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

18
00:02:00,343 --> 00:02:07,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody's going to, you know, not be able to help themselves in the beginning, but with... you just keep trying.

19
00:02:07,929 --> 00:02:17,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing the benefit of trying and getting stronger and stronger, and eventually you break them less and less, so to speak.

20
00:02:18,157 --> 00:02:20,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Could that person still go into seclusion?

21
00:02:21,779 --> 00:02:25,162
[SPEAKER_01]: They're trying their best, but, you know, failing every now and then?

22
00:02:25,672 --> 00:02:31,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, to the degree that they are not that lustful, that full of hate and that full of confusion, yeah.

23
00:02:32,535 --> 00:02:40,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I think, you know, seclusion is good for, you know, just looking at the mind, reflecting on the suttas, reflecting on these subjects.

24
00:02:42,655 --> 00:02:53,881
[SPEAKER_00]: A quiet time, not intense seclusion, but a quiet time of the day, a space where you can retire to, is good.

25
00:02:55,022 --> 00:03:02,445
[SPEAKER_00]: For any philosopher, anyone who wants to heal their mind, needs to meet that mind.

26
00:03:02,986 --> 00:03:11,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And the best place for meeting it is a place of... where there's no busyness, no others around.

27
00:03:14,575 --> 00:03:29,908
[SPEAKER_00]: When forests go for a walk even, a long, long walk, already a person who's, you know, just living a normal lay life, if they just go on the weekend to a forest, that already is, by themselves, contemplative.

28
00:03:30,528 --> 00:03:38,615
[SPEAKER_00]: You start to think about your life, what this, what you're going to do, you know, with your life, all the things that happen, you start rehashing the past and

29
00:03:40,653 --> 00:03:48,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Just just by that, a person can see that, just walking alone in a forest, you can already see how you start going inwards.

30
00:03:50,663 --> 00:03:51,524
[SPEAKER_00]: And looking at yourself.

31
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:53,486
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing.

32
00:03:53,526 --> 00:04:04,816
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can't face yourself, if you can't even just admit to the things that you did wrong in the past, that walk... you won't even be going to that forest for a walk on the weekend.

33
00:04:05,297 --> 00:04:05,997
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just too much.

34
00:04:06,017 --> 00:04:12,203
[SPEAKER_00]: You would have to have your friends with you, you would have to have your music playing in your ear, because you just can't handle yourself.

35
00:04:12,283 --> 00:04:16,167
[SPEAKER_00]: And that that type of person, they need to do a lot more work, actually.

